A||||5||Eddie||RADIATOR ? help||||11||
Z||000000||Eddie||12-02-1999||06:28 PM||||On my way to work today I looked down and I saw some green antifreeze on the ground right up front by the radiator (right hand side). So I took a fast look it looks to me like it could good be the hose or hose clamp. ps I hope its not the radiator. Has any one had this problem yet if so what was it. And is it norm. <BR>		Thanks EDDIE||||reg||11
Z||000001||Patrick||12-02-1999||06:35 PM||||<BR>I had a similar scare quite a while back. It turned out to be the small hose between the overflow tank and the radiator. I just ended up cutting an inch or so off the bad end of the hose, sticking it back on, and voila, no more leak! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||14
Z||000002||batboy||12-02-1999||08:39 PM||||Radiator hoses do get old and brittle. They're something that should be replaced more often then most people think. I'd warm it up to full operating temperature, then park it on clean pavement and watch for drips to figure out exactly where the coolant is leaking from.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Sage||12-02-1999||10:08 PM||||Put a preassure tester on it, it will show your leak right now!||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||12-03-1999||04:05 AM||||Other things that get old and brittle are the plastic tanks on the sides of the radiator. When they go bad you can drive yourself crazy trying to find it if its in a seam. It cost me over a $100 to replace the tank. Boy did they see me coming. Next time I'll take the money and just buy a metal 3 core radiator for almost the same price!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-03-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eddie||12-03-1999||08:17 PM||||Thanks guys Ill check it out Sat.EDDIE||||reg||7
